Resource:
One `resource_id` identifies an information object with unique content and version.
The same information may have different representation files, such as PDF, Markdown, TXT, OCR, or extracted text, and may have multiple source copies in non-public Library folders.
Preprints, published versions, revisions, substantive translations, or files with material additions or deletions must use different `resource_id` values.
Each Resource has only one canonical name, `canonical_name`; all other names are registered as `aliases`.
Upper-level knowledge-base resource tables use only `resource_id` as the Resource identity and may retain membership state and the original input for audit purposes. Entities, Relationships, and Graphs reference only `resource_id`, never resource file paths directly.
A unified Resource identity prevents references from drifting when files are renamed, moved, duplicated, or converted between formats.

Distinction from an Entity:
An Entity is an abstract knowledge object.
An `entity_id` is the name of that abstract object.
Different expressions of a piece of knowledge are abstract representations of the same knowledge Entity.
A Resource is the carrier of knowledge.
